The FIT checksum is the standard reflected CRC-16 with polynomial 0xA001 (CRC-16/ARC). The original code processes it 4 bits at a time, using two table lookups per byte.

This version processes a whole byte at once using a 256-entry table:

- **One lookup per byte:** each call does a single dependent lookup, `(crc >> 8) ^ table[(crc ^ byte) & 0xFF]`. The original did two dependent lookups per byte, so the per-byte dependency chain is roughly halved.
- **Same results for every input:** CRC arithmetic is linear over XOR, so the nibble-wise and byte-wise forms give the same CRC for any starting `crc` and any `byte`.
- **Table built at compile time:** the table is generated by a `constexpr` struct rather than typed in by hand, so there are no hand-copied constants to get wrong and no runtime initialization cost.

```cpp
FIT_UINT16 CRC::Get16(FIT_UINT16 crc, FIT_UINT8 byte)
{
   struct Table
   {
      FIT_UINT16 t[256];
      constexpr Table() : t()
      {
         for (unsigned i = 0; i < 256; i++)
         {
            unsigned c = i;
            for (int k = 0; k < 8; k++)
               c = (c & 1) ? ((c >> 1) ^ 0xA001u) : (c >> 1);
            t[i] = static_cast<FIT_UINT16>(c);
         }
      }
   };
   static constexpr Table table{};

   return static_cast<FIT_UINT16>((crc >> 8) ^ table.t[(crc ^ byte) & 0xFF]);
}
```